<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Which Game Modes Vessel supports, and what it promises in each.

  `supportsBatteryGameMode` and `supportsPerformanceGameMode` are the whole of
  the declaration: they are what makes the two entries appear in the Game
  Dashboard, and a mode not declared here is one the user cannot pick.

  `allowGameDownscaling="false"` in both, deliberately. The platform's downscale
  works by resizing the app's own surface, and Vessel's surface is a compositor
  for a guest that chose its own resolution — `display.resolution`, which the
  user set. Letting the system shrink it would fight that setting, and would not
  save the GPU anything anyway, because the expensive rendering happens inside
  the guest at the guest's resolution and is only then blitted. The honest lever
  for that is `display.resolution` itself.

  `allowGameFpsOverride="false"` for the same reason and a sharper one:
  `display.fpsLimit` now reaches DXVK and vkd3d as well as the compositor, so
  Vessel's own cap is the one that actually stops frames from being rendered. A
  platform FPS override applied on top would cap presentation a second time and
  reintroduce exactly the gap that was just measured and closed — frames
  rendered at full GPU cost and then dropped.

  What is left is the part worth having: with these declared, Performance mode
  tells the OEM power path to keep clocks up for this app, which is the thing
  that was missing when a Metro session ran at 1713 MHz of an available 3321.
-->
<game-mode-config
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:supportsBatteryGameMode="true"
    android:supportsPerformanceGameMode="true"
    android:allowGameDownscaling="false"
    android:allowGameFpsOverride="false" />
